From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 20:17:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] regulator: da9213-legacy: answer the VPROC1 enable check
 without I2C

On the MT6797 A53 station, a perf profile of the schedutil thread during
1080p video spent 13 % of its time in da9213_legacy_is_enabled(): the
regulator core checks the enable state on every voltage request, and each
check is an I2C6 transfer under the DVFS lease.

The driver has no enable operation, and BUCKA (VPROC1) feeds the CCI and
the CPU clusters the kernel runs on, so it cannot be off once a write to
it was verified. Answer 1 from then on, as the selector read already
does. BUCKB is still read from the chip.
---
 drivers/regulator/da9213-legacy-regulator.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/regulator/da9213-legacy-regulator.c b/drivers/regulator/da9213-legacy-regulator.c
index b5501872d..06f72be24 100644
--- a/drivers/regulator/da9213-legacy-regulator.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/da9213-legacy-regulator.c
@@ -224,12 +224,21 @@ static int da9213_legacy_get_voltage_sel(struct regulator_dev *rdev)
 	return value & DA9213_LEGACY_VSEL_MASK;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The driver has no enable operation, and BUCKA (VPROC1) feeds the CCI and the
+ * CPU clusters this kernel runs on, so it cannot be off once a write to it
+ * was verified. The regulator core asks on every voltage request; answer
+ * without an I2C transfer from then on. BUCKB is always read from the chip.
+ */
 static int da9213_legacy_is_enabled(struct regulator_dev *rdev)
 {
 	struct da9213_legacy *chip = rdev_get_drvdata(rdev);
 	unsigned int value;
 	int ret;
 
+	if (rdev_get_id(rdev) == 0 && chip->vproc1_known)
+		return 1;
+
 	ret = da9213_legacy_read_reg(chip,
 				     da9213_legacy_cont_reg[rdev_get_id(rdev)],
 				     &value);
-- 
2.43.0

